Description: Keep "/" inside an unquoted attribute value
 An unquoted attribute value ended at "/", so "href=/home" lost its value and
 "href=a/b" was read as "href=a". Following the HTML tokenizer's "attribute
 value (unquoted)" state, a "/" inside an unquoted value, or right after "=",
 is part of the value. A "/" straight before ">" is still the self-closing
 slash, as it is after a quoted value, so "<div class=a/>" and
 "<div class="a"/>" read the same: whether a tag closes itself never depends
 on how its last value is quoted. A browser would read "a/" there; no template
 in the corpus has that shape. An unquoted value that runs to the end of the
 input is kept instead of dropped.
Author: Numiko
Origin: vendor
Forwarded: no
Test: test/parser/parse-for-eslint.test.ts ("unquoted attribute values")
Last-Update: 2026-10-15
